{
 "$comment": "Authoritative cases for where a tap can actually land - read by BOTH the Kotlin (reticle-core TapReachContractTest) and Swift (ReticleProtocol TapReachContractTests) suites, so the Android helper and the iOS host cannot aim two different ways. A node's frame is its LAYOUT box, and a tap at the centre of that box is only correct while the whole box is reachable. What the cases pin: a fully visible node is tapped at its own centre and nothing is said; a node hanging off the bottom of the display is tapped on the part that is still on screen; a node laid out entirely off screen is REFUSED with the command that fixes it rather than dispatched at a coordinate no display has; the same two answers for a node clipped by a scroll container and for one scrolled out of it entirely; and the CSS rule for a DOM scroll port (a non-'visible' overflow clips its children) so a hybrid screen behaves the same as a native one. Measured on a device: a sheet row with frame y=2403 h=128 on a 2412px screen was dispatched at y=2468 and reported settled=1, and a row scrolled under a sticky header kept its full rect and sent the touch to the page behind the sheet.",
